  "text": "I'll read the brief and the guidance index first, then follow only the review path they name.The brief is a design review of login, setup, and notifications. I'll read the design method and React Bits guidance, then the pages and his notes.The method says to judge beside the accepted feeds and his renders. I'll open those images, the page code, and the history of what he loved and rejected.RESULT: FINDINGS\n\nThe host takes the smallest reading of each sentence and builds it as its own page. The circle kept the component's blue mark and three slogan chips (`p12-login.png`). Setup and Notifications are one panel on an empty ground (`p12-setup.png`, `p12-notifications.png`), the same shape as `landing-empty-sections-dark.png`. The feed and the finished onboarding are already one screen (`feed-open.png`, `onboarding-done.png`), in the family of `accepted-deck-dark.png`.\n\n1. He names the real object. The page gets a stand-in.\n\"you've put oparax's blue logo. If I say okay to that also, you could have put multiple different social media around it in the circle, right, or you could have put the card there. Are you stupid?\" The circle's sample centre and its trust chips stayed, renamed \"One story per event\", \"Sources you chose\", \"Alerts on X\".\nHe had already said what makes a page alive: \"if it weren't for you adding the Vercel, Hugging Face, and all those logos on the left, it would have never looked so lively.\"\n\"There just needs to be one visual UI.\" Setup is still its own card. \"There's no difference between the building page and the setup page.\"\n\"Notifications are fine, but I guess now that we're introducing the sidebar or the header, it'll change a bit,\" and \"that card itself doesn't look good.\" Fine was treated as finished.\nSame habit earlier: \"you only looked at the elements I told you about.\" After the strip-down: \"you removed a bunch of other cards, so this page is lifeless. I didn't want that.\"\n\n2. The centre is one real story card, \"Next.js 15 is released as stable\", about 220px wide, the picture on top, the title and the Next.js line under it.\nOn the dashed ring (about 340px, one turn in 90 seconds, still when motion is reduced): four marks, 36px, evenly spaced: X, RSS, GitHub, and the website globe.\nOn the outer ring (about 480px, still): six real marks from the sample, 40px, no words under them: Next.js, Vercel, Hugging Face, Cursor, Mistral AI, Simon Willison.\nThe rings stay. The mark in the header is the Oparax mark for this page.\nThose sources sit around the story the agent writes.\n\n3. Setup is the onboarding page before the run: the same three columns, steps, sources, and brief.\nThe top bar is one line. The heading \"Set up your agent\", then the handle field, then the sentence as one line, placeholder \"One sentence. Name the topics, people or products you care about.\" Build my agent sits at the right end. That side button is the right place.\nUnder it, quiet placeholders. The eight steps, unmarked. The labels X accounts, RSS feeds, and Websites, no cards and no counts. The words \"Your brief\", empty, so the sentence is only in the bar.\nBuild starts the run on this page. The heading becomes Choosing sources, then Saving your agent, then Your agent is ready.\nOnce the run starts, the fields lock. The handle becomes the X account chip already on that bar. The sentence moves into Your brief on the right.\n\n4. It is one row in a void. \"X DMs\" is a label with no neighbour, the bordered button is the only action, and the panel is 760px wide while Settings is 640px and Sources is a tall list.\nThere is no second channel to show, so there is no Email row and no \"Soon\" row.\nPut the row in the Settings panel, the same width and row height as Account and Plan, between Plan and Sign out. The Notifications route stays and shows that same panel.\nThe row holds the X logo, the title X DMs, one line, \"Oparax messages @farzanmrz on X when a story matters.\", and a blue Message @oparax_ai at the right.\nThe extra instruction line goes. The button says that.\n\n5. When he names a real object and a place, put that object in the frame he already accepted, and let nothing in a component's sample stand in for it.",
